 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**The advertised standard variable rate for owner-occupiers is about 4.4 percentage points above the cash rate.** In September 2026 it was 8.77%, while the cash rate target averaged 4.36% over the month.\n\n| Month | Cash rate (monthly average) | Standard variable | Gap | Discounted variable | Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Jun 2025 | 3.85% | 8.27% | 4.42 pts | 6.30% | 2.45 pts |\n| Dec 2025 | 3.60% | 8.02% | 4.42 pts | 6.05% | 2.45 pts |\n| Sep 2026 | 4.36% | 8.77% | 4.41 pts | 6.80% | 2.44 pts |\n\n- **The gap has stayed the same.** Banks have passed on the cash rate changes in full in both directions. That includes the cuts in late 2025 and the roughly 0.75-point rise in February–May 2026. So the standard variable rate has moved almost exactly in step with the cash rate.\n- **The cash rate figure is a monthly average.** September's 4.36% (against 4.35% in the months before) could mean the target changed late in the month. The lending rate figures don't yet show any change.\n- **Few borrowers pay the standard rate.** The advertised discounted variable rate is a better guide, at 6.80%, or about 2.4 points over the cash rate. The rates actually paid on new loans are usually lower again.\n\nSources: RBA tables F1.1 (cash rate) and F5 (advertised lending rates), to September 2026.",
